1
00:00:02.002 --> 00:00:05.605
This last lesson is an intro to haystacks
core abstractions.

2
00:00:05.605 --> 00:00:06.006
You'll learn

3
00:00:06.006 --> 00:00:09.809
what components are, how they work, and
how they can be combined into pipelines.

4
00:00:09.809 --> 00:00:12.879
So many are use cases
as well as what documents

5
00:00:12.879 --> 00:00:16.016
stores are and how they can be accessed
by a pipeline.

6
00:00:16.016 --> 00:00:19.719
We'll start by creating a simple
indexing pipeline followed by a token

7
00:00:19.719 --> 00:00:22.322
search pipeline. Let's dive in.

8
00:00:22.322 --> 00:00:25.191
AI applications are often
made up of multiple steps

9
00:00:25.191 --> 00:00:27.427
that work together to achieve a goal.

10
00:00:27.427 --> 00:00:28.762
For example, retrieval.

11
00:00:28.762 --> 00:00:30.397
Augmented generation.

12
00:00:30.397 --> 00:00:32.198
This is usually made up of two steps.

13
00:00:32.198 --> 00:00:35.535
The retrieval step that is,
looking at a database

14
00:00:35.535 --> 00:00:39.005
and extracting the most relevant documents
from that database.

15
00:00:39.005 --> 00:00:41.541
And then the generation step
that's using the context

16
00:00:41.541 --> 00:00:44.644
from these documents
to generate a response.

17
00:00:44.644 --> 00:00:46.913
But you may have to
sometimes switch things up.

18
00:00:46.913 --> 00:00:51.913
For example, you may have to add a ranking
step between retrieval and generation.

19
00:00:52.619 --> 00:00:54.521
In a full AI application,

20
00:00:54.521 --> 00:00:59.225
there are smaller tasks that are combined
into a larger use case in haystack.

21
00:00:59.259 --> 00:01:02.729
All of these small tasks are achieved
by components.

22
00:01:02.729 --> 00:01:07.167
A component is then combined
with other components to form a pipeline.

23
00:01:07.167 --> 00:01:09.102
These pipelines are the entities

24
00:01:09.102 --> 00:01:13.206
that are achieving an application
that we want to build.

25
00:01:13.206 --> 00:01:16.576
A pipeline
also has access to some databases.

26
00:01:16.609 --> 00:01:18.778
In haystack we call these document stores.

27
00:01:18.778 --> 00:01:19.579
And the pipeline

28
00:01:19.579 --> 00:01:23.683
has access to these document stores
through some of the components.

29
00:01:23.683 --> 00:01:27.954
For example, you may store data
and we V8 quadrant or MongoDB

30
00:01:27.954 --> 00:01:32.058
and have your pipeline access
these documents through some components.

31
00:01:32.058 --> 00:01:34.594
Pipelines are composed
by connecting these components

32
00:01:34.594 --> 00:01:37.597
in a way that achieve an AI application.

33
00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:40.233
But a component
can expect any number of inputs,

34
00:01:40.233 --> 00:01:43.536
and it can also produce
any number of outputs.

35
00:01:43.536 --> 00:01:46.973
For example, the sentence Transformers
document embedded.

36
00:01:46.973 --> 00:01:51.444
This component expects a list of documents
and returns the same list of documents

37
00:01:51.444 --> 00:01:52.779
enriched with embeddings.

38
00:01:52.779 --> 00:01:55.215
It also returns some metadata.

39
00:01:55.215 --> 00:01:58.451
This component uses sentence transformers
embedding models

40
00:01:58.451 --> 00:02:00.753
to create these embeddings.

41
00:02:00.753 --> 00:02:01.754
For example, let's

42
00:02:01.754 --> 00:02:05.024
start with a sentence
is Taylor Swift Queen of the world?

43
00:02:05.024 --> 00:02:08.027
We may have a component,
let's say an embedding

44
00:02:08.194 --> 00:02:11.030
that produces an embedding for this query.

45
00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:14.834
We may have another component
that's expecting an embedding or a vector.

46
00:02:14.834 --> 00:02:17.170
And it's then producing a document.

47
00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:18.905
Let's call this a retriever.

48
00:02:18.905 --> 00:02:23.309
If we then combine these two components
we've achieved a pretty accurate document

49
00:02:23.309 --> 00:02:25.111
search pipeline. For example.

50
00:02:25.111 --> 00:02:27.280
Haystack has many ready made components.

51
00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:28.848
For example, generators

52
00:02:28.848 --> 00:02:32.752
that access different model
providers, embeddings that does the same.

53
00:02:32.752 --> 00:02:36.723
We have different retrievers that are able
to retrieve from many databases.

54
00:02:36.723 --> 00:02:38.925
We have converters, but the list goes on.

55
00:02:38.925 --> 00:02:42.395
you may have rankers,
routers, preprocessors and so on.

56
00:02:42.395 --> 00:02:45.632
We build pipelines to create applications
like question

57
00:02:45.632 --> 00:02:50.069
answering, document search chats, question
generation, output validation.

58
00:02:50.069 --> 00:02:52.705
And the list can go on and on.

59
00:02:52.705 --> 00:02:54.741
We build these pipelines with haystack.

60
00:02:54.741 --> 00:02:57.410
But haystack
pipelines can also branch out.

61
00:02:57.410 --> 00:03:02.410
That means we can have locations
that have a decision making component.

62
00:03:02.749 --> 00:03:05.018
Pipelines can get quite complex.

63
00:03:05.018 --> 00:03:09.589
Haystack pipelines can also loop
so it loops until a certain case is met

64
00:03:09.589 --> 00:03:10.924
and so on.

65
00:03:10.924 --> 00:03:14.460
But the most important thing
is, if haystack doesn't have a component

66
00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:18.531
that you need to build your application,
you can go ahead and build your own

67
00:03:18.531 --> 00:03:22.068
and put those into the pipeline
where they make sense.

68
00:03:22.068 --> 00:03:23.903
All right. Let's see all this in code.

69
00:03:23.903 --> 00:03:28.903
And let's start using haystack
components, pipelines and document stores.

70
00:03:29.042 --> 00:03:31.411
You'll first start by adding this line

71
00:03:31.411 --> 00:03:34.414
to suppress any warnings
that we don't need.

72
00:03:34.514 --> 00:03:37.884
Next, we're going to use a helper function

73
00:03:37.884 --> 00:03:41.688
that will allow us to import
all of the environment variables

74
00:03:41.688 --> 00:03:46.688
that we're going to need for this lab,
such as OpenAI API keys, and so on.

75
00:03:46.993 --> 00:03:47.694
Now let's

76
00:03:47.694 --> 00:03:51.698
start creating our first component
and using it.

77
00:03:51.698 --> 00:03:56.236
The first component that you'll use
is the OpenAI document and beta.

78
00:03:56.236 --> 00:04:01.236
This component uses OpenAI embedding
models to create embeddings for documents.

79
00:04:01.507 --> 00:04:04.477
First,
you'll import the document embeddings

80
00:04:04.477 --> 00:04:07.413
and then you'll initialize
a document in beta.

81
00:04:07.413 --> 00:04:10.817
In this case,
you'll be using text embedding three

82
00:04:10.817 --> 00:04:12.986
small as your embedding model.

83
00:04:12.986 --> 00:04:16.055
You can also inspect what kind of inputs

84
00:04:16.055 --> 00:04:19.525
and outputs this component is expecting.

85
00:04:19.525 --> 00:04:21.661
For example, we can see that

86
00:04:21.661 --> 00:04:25.031
this component
is expecting a list of documents as input.

87
00:04:25.031 --> 00:04:29.269
And it's going to be producing documents
and matter as output.

88
00:04:29.269 --> 00:04:32.138
We already know that
this is an embedding component.

89
00:04:32.138 --> 00:04:37.138
So the documents that it's going to be
producing will also have embeddings.

90
00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:41.381
Now let's see how
we would run this component.

91
00:04:41.381 --> 00:04:44.284
For this we've created some dummy
documents.

92
00:04:44.284 --> 00:04:46.286
have a list of two documents.

93
00:04:46.286 --> 00:04:49.789
And each of these documents
are mentioning things about haystack

94
00:04:49.789 --> 00:04:54.227
and how you can build AI applications
with haystack.

95
00:04:54.227 --> 00:04:58.564
Because we know the embedded component
is expecting documents as input.

96
00:04:58.564 --> 00:05:01.567
We can run it with these documents.

97
00:05:01.567 --> 00:05:04.771
When we run this component,
you'll notice that it's produced

98
00:05:04.804 --> 00:05:07.006
a list of documents as outputs.

99
00:05:07.006 --> 00:05:09.575
And the meta information
is also telling us

100
00:05:09.575 --> 00:05:13.079
what kind of model
was used to create these documents.

101
00:05:13.079 --> 00:05:18.017
And the vectors of size 1536.

102
00:05:18.017 --> 00:05:21.254
Now that you know how
to use a component on its own,

103
00:05:21.254 --> 00:05:24.257
let's
see how we would use it in a pipeline.

104
00:05:24.257 --> 00:05:27.560
First, we're going to start
with initializing a document store.

105
00:05:27.627 --> 00:05:31.164
And then we're going to build a pipeline
that writes documents

106
00:05:31.164 --> 00:05:34.400
alongside the embeddings
into a document store.

107
00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:37.904
For now we're going to be using
the in-memory document store.

108
00:05:37.904 --> 00:05:40.773
This is the simplest document store
you can use in haystack,

109
00:05:40.773 --> 00:05:43.209
and it has no requirements to use it.

110
00:05:43.209 --> 00:05:46.546
But if you like,
you can switch this to any documents store

111
00:05:46.546 --> 00:05:51.546
such as quadrant V, V8, pinecone,
Croma, and the list goes on.

112
00:05:51.984 --> 00:05:54.287
Now we have an in-memory document store.

113
00:05:54.287 --> 00:05:58.958
Let's write our first txt file
into this document store.

114
00:05:58.958 --> 00:06:02.061
We're going to start
by importing all of the component s

115
00:06:02.061 --> 00:06:06.933
you're going to be using
for the first indexing pipeline.

116
00:06:06.933 --> 00:06:10.069
You're going to be using the open
AI document embedded again.

117
00:06:10.069 --> 00:06:15.069
But you're also going to be using
some preprocessors and converters as well.

118
00:06:15.775 --> 00:06:17.777
For this demo, we're going to be using

119
00:06:17.777 --> 00:06:21.214
all of the default variables
for these components.

120
00:06:21.214 --> 00:06:24.550
But you can also switch this up.

121
00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:27.053
We'll start with a converter
because we're going to have a.

122
00:06:27.053 --> 00:06:28.888
TXT file about Da Vinci

123
00:06:28.888 --> 00:06:32.392
that we're going to be writing
into our in-memory document store.

124
00:06:32.392 --> 00:06:35.962
Next we're going to be using the document
splitter

125
00:06:35.962 --> 00:06:39.332
The document splitter is a component
that chunks up your document.

126
00:06:39.332 --> 00:06:42.201
By default. It splits by 200 words.

127
00:06:42.201 --> 00:06:44.737
And we're going to be using this default
setup.

128
00:06:44.737 --> 00:06:46.906
However, if you like you can change this.

129
00:06:46.906 --> 00:06:50.643
for example you can say split by

130
00:06:50.643 --> 00:06:55.643
and decide to split by passage instead.

131
00:06:57.016 --> 00:06:58.551
This will split your document

132
00:06:58.551 --> 00:07:03.523
by 200 paragraphs, for example,
but let's use the default values.

133
00:07:03.523 --> 00:07:05.758
Next you're going to be using them better.

134
00:07:05.758 --> 00:07:09.529
In this case we're going to be using
the OpenAI document in beta.

135
00:07:09.529 --> 00:07:13.633
The final component you're going to be
using is a document writer.

136
00:07:13.633 --> 00:07:15.368
You already have a document store.

137
00:07:15.368 --> 00:07:18.404
So here you'll be telling the document
writer that it should be

138
00:07:18.404 --> 00:07:21.841
writing
into your in-memory document store.

139
00:07:21.841 --> 00:07:25.278
Now that you have all of your components,
the next thing you have to do

140
00:07:25.278 --> 00:07:29.782
is create a pipeline
and add these components to that pipeline.

141
00:07:29.782 --> 00:07:32.718
You do this by initializing your pipeline,

142
00:07:32.718 --> 00:07:35.721
and next you'll be adding each component.

143
00:07:35.788 --> 00:07:38.891
The important thing here to note
is for every component,

144
00:07:38.891 --> 00:07:40.359
you'll be providing a name,

145
00:07:40.359 --> 00:07:42.628
you can name your component
anything you want,

146
00:07:42.628 --> 00:07:45.231
but then you have to make sure
that you're using this name.

147
00:07:45.231 --> 00:07:47.767
Next.

148
00:07:47.767 --> 00:07:50.970
Now that you've added the components,
your pipeline,

149
00:07:50.970 --> 00:07:53.606
the pipeline has access to the components,
but it doesn't

150
00:07:53.606 --> 00:07:57.944
actually know how these components
interact with each other yet.

151
00:07:57.944 --> 00:08:02.048
So this haystack uses
component connections.

152
00:08:02.048 --> 00:08:06.619
For example you'll start by connecting
the converter to the splitter.

153
00:08:06.619 --> 00:08:10.189
This is basically telling the pipeline
that the output of converter

154
00:08:10.189 --> 00:08:13.025
should be handed over to the splitter.

155
00:08:13.025 --> 00:08:16.295
Let's connect
all of our other components as well.

156
00:08:16.295 --> 00:08:19.165
And when you run this,
you'll also get to see

157
00:08:19.165 --> 00:08:22.835
what kind of connections
you've created in your pipeline.

158
00:08:22.835 --> 00:08:26.105
Once you've connected
all of your components in the pipeline,

159
00:08:26.105 --> 00:08:30.309
you'll also be able to observe
what components your pipeline has access

160
00:08:30.309 --> 00:08:34.380
to and exactly what the connections
between these components are.

161
00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:39.380
So we're basically seeing that converters
documents output specifically

162
00:08:39.485 --> 00:08:43.589
is being inputted
into splitter documents input.

163
00:08:43.589 --> 00:08:44.557
You'll remember from

164
00:08:44.557 --> 00:08:48.794
before that the embedding
was expecting documents as input.

165
00:08:48.794 --> 00:08:52.398
So it's being given documents
by the splitter.

166
00:08:52.398 --> 00:08:54.433
But it's also producing documents.

167
00:08:54.433 --> 00:08:56.769
Only this time it has embeddings.

168
00:08:56.769 --> 00:09:01.440
So the documents output of the embedding
is now being given to the writer.

169
00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:02.742
Documents input.

170
00:09:02.742 --> 00:09:03.843
Another utility

171
00:09:03.843 --> 00:09:08.748
that haystack provides is a way
to visualize these pipelines as well.

172
00:09:08.748 --> 00:09:12.785
You simply have to call
that show on your pipeline.

173
00:09:12.785 --> 00:09:15.354
And you'll get a graph of exactly

174
00:09:15.354 --> 00:09:19.492
what your pipeline looks like,
including all of the connections.

175
00:09:19.492 --> 00:09:23.629
In this case, we know that
our pipeline starts with a converter.

176
00:09:23.629 --> 00:09:27.767
And the thing
the input that it's expecting are sources.

177
00:09:27.767 --> 00:09:30.436
Now that you have your pipeline
and you know that

178
00:09:30.436 --> 00:09:32.572
the connections are accurate.

179
00:09:32.572 --> 00:09:34.340
You can try to run it.

180
00:09:34.340 --> 00:09:35.308
You already saw that

181
00:09:35.308 --> 00:09:39.545
the first component in that pipeline
was the converter component,

182
00:09:39.745 --> 00:09:43.683
and that it expects a list of sources
for this lab.

183
00:09:43.716 --> 00:09:44.817
We have a.

184
00:09:44.817 --> 00:09:49.817
TXT file about DaVinci which will be using
to index into in-memory documents store.

185
00:09:52.391 --> 00:09:56.295
So we call run on our indexing pipeline.

186
00:09:56.295 --> 00:10:01.295
And we tell the run method that
the converter is expecting inputs sources.

187
00:10:01.701 --> 00:10:06.701
And we're also providing
at the location of our txt file.

188
00:10:06.772 --> 00:10:10.576
We run this pipeline and you'll see that
it's calculating the embeddings

189
00:10:10.576 --> 00:10:14.547
with the default embedding model we have
with the OpenAI document embeddings.

190
00:10:14.814 --> 00:10:16.916
And it's also letting us know that it's

191
00:10:16.916 --> 00:10:21.187
written 47 documents
into our in-memory document store.

192
00:10:21.187 --> 00:10:21.854
To check.

193
00:10:21.854 --> 00:10:25.992
You can also inspect
your document store as is now.

194
00:10:25.992 --> 00:10:29.895
For example, we can filter
our document store with no filter

195
00:10:29.895 --> 00:10:34.895
and simply inspect the contents
of the document at index five.

196
00:10:35.635 --> 00:10:37.370
And here we have it.

197
00:10:37.370 --> 00:10:40.706
Now that you've created
your in-memory document store

198
00:10:40.706 --> 00:10:44.710
with documents about DaVinci
as well as the embeddings,

199
00:10:44.710 --> 00:10:48.214
you can create your first document
search pipeline.

200
00:10:48.214 --> 00:10:52.251
So this will first import all of the
components we're going to be using.

201
00:10:52.251 --> 00:10:56.389
And then we start creating the components
that we want to use.

202
00:10:56.389 --> 00:11:00.059
The important thing here is that
because we use the OpenAI

203
00:11:00.059 --> 00:11:03.062
document embedding
with the default embedding model,

204
00:11:03.162 --> 00:11:07.733
we know we're going to have to use
the same model to embed the incoming query

205
00:11:07.733 --> 00:11:10.469
from the user for this use case.

206
00:11:10.469 --> 00:11:13.706
Then we're going to be using the OpenAI
text in beta.

207
00:11:13.706 --> 00:11:16.108
And we're calling this aquarium better.

208
00:11:16.108 --> 00:11:18.110
Next we need a retriever.

209
00:11:18.110 --> 00:11:20.012
We use the in-memory document store.

210
00:11:20.012 --> 00:11:24.350
So for this case we're going to be using
the in-memory embedding retriever

211
00:11:24.350 --> 00:11:27.520
telling it to retrieve documents
from our documents, store.

212
00:11:27.520 --> 00:11:30.856
Next, we initialize our pipeline.

213
00:11:30.856 --> 00:11:33.359
We call this pipeline document search,

214
00:11:33.359 --> 00:11:37.530
and we simply add the components
we have to that pipeline.

215
00:11:37.530 --> 00:11:41.734
Finally, as we did before, we're going
to be connecting our components.

216
00:11:41.734 --> 00:11:45.738
I'll start with a connection
that's actually going to be incorrect

217
00:11:45.738 --> 00:11:49.041
or it's not very clear
exactly what the connections are.

218
00:11:49.041 --> 00:11:53.713
So if you run this,
you'll notice that you get an error.

219
00:11:53.713 --> 00:11:56.682
These
types of errors are actually quite useful.

220
00:11:56.682 --> 00:12:00.519
This is telling us that there's a pipeline
connection error because it doesn't know

221
00:12:00.519 --> 00:12:03.989
exactly how the query embeddings
should be connected to the retriever,

222
00:12:03.989 --> 00:12:07.893
because there are multiple ways
that these two components could connect.

223
00:12:07.893 --> 00:12:12.765
It also gives us some recommendations
of what these connections could look like,

224
00:12:12.765 --> 00:12:16.936
and lets us know what the outputs
and inputs of both components

225
00:12:16.936 --> 00:12:21.841
are To resolve this,
we simply have to let the pipeline know

226
00:12:21.841 --> 00:12:26.841
that specifically the embedding output
from the query and better

227
00:12:26.979 --> 00:12:31.979
should be given to the query
embedding input of the retriever.

228
00:12:33.319 --> 00:12:34.120
And there we are.

229
00:12:34.120 --> 00:12:37.223
We now have a document search pipeline.

230
00:12:37.223 --> 00:12:40.192
Again, you can use the show utility

231
00:12:40.192 --> 00:12:44.563
to make sure that you've created
the pipeline that you expect to see.

232
00:12:44.563 --> 00:12:45.097
So you can

233
00:12:45.097 --> 00:12:49.101
see that the first component
query embedding is expecting text.

234
00:12:49.101 --> 00:12:53.539
This is going to be the question,
for example, that the user is asking.

235
00:12:53.539 --> 00:12:58.043
This component is then going to output
embeddings to the retriever,

236
00:12:58.043 --> 00:13:01.914
which is then going to return
the most relevant documents.

237
00:13:01.914 --> 00:13:04.350
Let's run our document search pipeline.

238
00:13:04.350 --> 00:13:09.350
Let's start with the question
how old was da Vinci when he died?

